# Conditional Card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The Conditional Card shows another card only while its conditions are true. In YAML it is `type: conditional`. Use it to keep a dashboard short: a frost warning that appears only when it is cold, or a card that appears only on a small screen.

It does not draw anything of its own. People see only the card inside it, or nothing.

This page covers:

- Conditional Card
  - `hui-conditional-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A list of conditions.
- One wrapped card, set in `card`.

When every condition is true, the wrapped card appears where the Conditional Card sits and takes its size. When any condition is false, the card is removed from the page. While you edit the dashboard, the card stays visible so you can still edit it.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-conditional-card"><code>hui-conditional-card</code></h3>

```yaml
type: conditional
conditions:
  - condition: numeric_state
    entity: sensor.outside_temperature
    below: 3
card:
  type: markdown
  content: The outside temperature is below 3°C. Check that the pipes are protected.
```

Keys:

- `conditions` is required and must be a list. Home Assistant shows “No conditions configured” or “Conditions need to be an array” when it is missing or the wrong shape.
- `card` is required. Home Assistant shows “No card configured” when it is missing.

Condition types that the dashboard checks itself:

- `state`: an entity, or one of its attributes, equals a state (`state`) or does not (`state_not`).
- `numeric_state`: a number is `above` or `below` a value.
- `screen`: a CSS media query matches, such as a narrow screen.
- `user`: the signed-in person is one of a list of users.
- `view_columns`: the view has at least `min` or at most `max` columns.
- `location`: the person’s device is in or away from a zone.
- `time`: the time is `after` or `before` a time, on chosen weekdays.
- `and`, `or` and `not`: combine other conditions.

The older form `entity` with `state` or `state_not`, with no `condition` key, still works. Conditions such as `template`, `sun`, `zone` and conditions from integrations are accepted and checked by Home Assistant itself.

The card has no theme keys of its own, so nothing in the Civica theme changes it. The card inside it follows its own page: its colours, focus and layout are unchanged.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It keeps a dashboard short by showing a card only when it is useful.
- A hidden card leaves no placeholder and no message. Nobody can tell that something is missing.
- Conditions on screen size, users and locations are checked in the browser. They change what is drawn and are not security. Anyone with access to the dashboard can still read its configuration.
- The card is not announced when it appears or disappears. Do not promise it.
- Focus is not moved to a card that appears.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- Keep the layout predictable. A card that jumps in and out moves everything below it.
- Do not hide a card that carries safety information, such as an alarm, a smoke sensor or a leak warning, behind a condition that can fail silently. If the sensor is unavailable the condition may be false and the warning disappears.
- If it matters that something is missing, say so in a visible card: “Frost warning: no reading from the outside sensor.”
- Do not hide controls that people rely on every day.
- Use clear conditions and few of them. Combine more than two with `and` or `or` only when you have to.
- Do not use it to hide private information from other people in the house. Use Home Assistant’s user permissions and see [Presence, people and shared-screen privacy](../home-assistant/presence-and-privacy.html).

Success criteria that apply:

- 4.1.3 Status Messages: a card that appears is not announced. Put important changes in a status that is.
- 2.4.3 Focus Order and 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the wrapped card sits in the page order where the Conditional Card is.
- 3.2.2 On Input and 3.2.4 Consistent Identification: appearing and disappearing must not surprise people.

